 dict(pmid='43015098', doi='10.1136/thorax-2026-225905', journal='Thorax', title='Air pollution and lung cancer survival: moving from association to population-level benefit.', short='Christiani Thorax editorial air pollution lung cancer survival population-level benefit', design='Metadata only (no abstract)', pm='Metadata only', geo='Not stated in record', endpoint='Metadata only', tier='C', basis='Review / no primary exposure', affil='Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health, USA; Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health, USA', n='Metadata only: no abstract (editorial). Thorax editorial by Christiani on air pollution and lung cancer survival, framed around moving from association to population-level benefit; presumably accompanies a research paper in the same issue, which is not identified in the record. No finding is asserted here. Relevance: peripheral - locate the index paper in the weekly sweep.', sub=corpus.RESP),
 dict(pmid='43014220', doi='10.1016/j.ecoenv.2026.121023', journal='Ecotoxicol Environ Saf', title='How prenatal residential greenness links to congenital heart defects: The role of multi-pollutant mediating pathways and non-linear exposure-response.', short='Xiamen 890 matched pairs prenatal greenness NDVI landscape indices congenital heart defects NO2 PM2.5 mediation', design='Case-control', pm='First-trimester NDVI (250-1000 m) and landscape metrics; NO2, PM2.5, SO2, O3 as mediators', geo='China (Xiamen)', endpoint='Congenital heart defects; indirect effects via air pollutants', tier='B', basis='Not stated', affil="Key Laboratory of Urban Environment and Health, CAS, China; Women and Children's Hospital, Xiamen University, China", n='Xiamen birth records 2016-2019: 1,785 CHD cases and 1,848 controls, 890 pairs after 1:1 propensity matching. First-trimester greenness was associated with lower CHD odds; at 1 km, large core patches and connectivity were protective and fragmentation and edge density harmful. Multi-pollutant mediation found indirect paths through NO2 and PM2.5, largest for NO2; the SO2 path did not survive joint modelling and the O3 indirect estimate was positive. The NDVI-NO2 relation turned at NDVI about 0.46. No effect sizes are given in the abstract, the PM2.5 exposure model is not stated, and propensity matching discarded half the cases. Relevance: indirect - PM2.5 enters only as a mediator of greenness.', sub=corpus.REPR),
 dict(pmid='43011089', doi='10.1007/s10694-026-02032-9', journal='Fire Technol', title='Potential Airborne Particulate Matter (PM) Exposures for Firefighters Working Outside Live-Fire Training Structures.', short='UL FSRI NIOSH live-fire training structures exterior real-time optical PM instructors fuels location', design='Field measurement', pm='Stationary time-resolved optical real-time PM area measurements outside two live-fire training structures', geo='USA', endpoint='Exterior PM concentrations by fuel load, structure and staging location', tier='B', basis='Lab / research instrument', affil='Fire Safety Research Institute, UL Research Institutes, USA; NIOSH, USA', n='Stationary optical real-time PM area monitors outside two training structures: a single-storey fire-behaviour lab (five fuels) and a two-storey concrete multicompartment structure (three fuels). In the fire-behaviour lab, fuel load had limited effect on exterior PM, but standing upwind and further from the structure lowered the hazard; at the multicompartment structure fuel load mattered more and the second floor showed high PM where instructors may be unable to relocate. No concentrations, instrument model or smoke-specific correction are given in the abstract; area monitors are not personal breathing-zone exposure. Relevance: indirect - optical monitors in fresh smoke need a smoke-specific mass correction before such numbers are compared with limits.', sub=corpus.OCC),
 dict(pmid='43011044', doi='10.1051/e3sconf/202671605024', journal='Int Conf Indoor Air Qual Vent Energy Conserv Build', title='Associations between night-time bedroom environmental factors and objective sleep metrics of older adults.', short='OHSU Portland 29 older adults bedroom PM2.5 light noise temperature humidity TVOC CO2 objective sleep 211 nights', design='Long-term indoor monitoring campaign', pm='Night-averaged bedroom PM2.5 among seven in-home environmental sensors', geo='USA (Portland, Oregon)', endpoint='Total sleep time, WASO, sleep efficiency, movement, heart and respiratory rate', tier='C', basis='Not stated', affil='Oregon Health and Science University, USA; Oregon Health and Science University, USA', n='29 older adults (17 cognitively normal, 12 with MCI or dementia; mean age about 77) followed for a mean 211 nights at home with objective sleep metrics and seven bedroom environmental measures. Light had the strongest association with total sleep time, humidity with WASO and efficiency, temperature with movement and heart and respiratory rate; PM2.5 was associated with total sleep time (effect size -0.050) and sleep efficiency (-0.058), Bonferroni significant. The PM2.5 sensor is not identified, effect sizes are standardised without stated units, and 29 people give many nights but little between-person information. Conference paper. Relevance: direct - long-run in-home PM sensing paired with passive physiology is the deployment model for indoor exposure-response work.', sub=corpus.OCC),
